<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>How fire and smoke damage appraisal in South Carolina works</h2>
<p>Fire and smoke damage appraisal in South Carolina is used when the insurer accepts the fire loss but the parties disagree on cost. Fire claims tend to be large and emotionally exhausting, and the gap between the carrier’s estimate and a contractor’s bid can be substantial, especially for smoke and soot that traveled well beyond the burned area.</p>
<p>Fires start from kitchens, electrical faults, dryers, heating equipment and, frequently in summer, lightning strikes. Lightning can ignite attics, damage wiring and electronics, and leave little visible damage while causing expensive hidden problems.</p>
<h2>What a complete fire and smoke scope covers</h2>
<ul>
<li>Structural repairs in the burn area, including framing, sheathing, roofing and electrical.</li>
<li>Smoke and soot cleaning or replacement in rooms outside the burn area.</li>
<li>Odor control: sealing, deodorization and HVAC duct cleaning or replacement.</li>
<li>Water damage from firefighting, which can be extensive.</li>
<li>Contents cleaning, inventory and replacement.</li>
<li>Additional living expense while the home is uninhabitable.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Smoke travels</b>Smoke moves through attics, wall cavities, return ducts and closets. A room that looks clean can hold odor that returns on humid days. Testing and careful inspection help prove how far it went.</div>
<h2>Common fire claim estimate disputes</h2>
<h3>Clean or replace</h3>
<p>Carriers often propose cleaning and sealing surfaces, insulation and contents that the owner believes must be replaced. Porous materials, such as insulation, carpet pad and upholstered furniture, are frequent sticking points.</p>
<h3>Wiring and code upgrades</h3>
<p>Heat can damage wiring well beyond visible charring. Rebuilding a burned section of an older South Carolina home can also trigger current electrical and building code requirements, and how ordinance or law coverage applies matters a great deal.</p>
<h3>Contents valuation</h3>
<p>Replacement cost versus actual cash value, depreciation rates, and whether items can truly be cleaned are all common disagreements. A detailed inventory with ages and prices helps enormously.</p>
<h3>Historic and specialty construction</h3>
<p>Charleston single houses, Beaufort antebellum homes and older churches may have masonry, plaster and millwork that require specialty trades. Rebuilding with modern materials may not be acceptable to local review boards.</p>
<h2>Documentation that strengthens a fire appraisal</h2>
<p>Gather the fire department report, photos and video of every room, including those that seem untouched, your contents inventory, receipts for emergency board-up and lodging, and any testing reports for soot or odor. Keep contractor bids and specialty cleaner proposals. Our <a href="/free-claim-tools/claim-gap-fee-estimator/">claim gap and fee estimator</a> can give you a rough sense of whether the gap justifies appraisal.</p>
<h2>How a fire appraisal panel usually works</h2>
<p>Fire panels often begin with a joint walk-through to agree on the boundary between rooms that need structural repair, rooms that need cleaning and rooms that need replacement of finishes. With that map settled, the appraisers can work through pricing room by room. Contents are usually handled as a separate schedule, with agreed ages, condition and replacement prices for each item.</p>
<p>Because fire claims are large, we keep a running list of agreed and disputed items so that progress is visible. If an umpire is needed, that list keeps the umpire focused on the items that are truly open rather than relitigating the whole loss.</p>
<h2>When appraisal is not the right tool for a fire claim</h2>
<p>If the insurer is investigating the cause of the fire, questioning whether the policy applies, or has raised concerns about the claim itself, the issue is coverage, not amount, and you should speak with a South Carolina attorney before doing anything else. Appraisal also doesn’t address delays in handling or payment. Questions about how a claim is being handled can be raised with the SCDOI.</p>
<p>If coverage is settled and the dispute is purely about cost, appraisal can be an efficient way to finish a difficult claim.</p>

<h2>Questions about fire &amp; smoke damage appraisal</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Can appraisal decide whether smoke-damaged items are replaced or cleaned?</summary><div class="a"><p>Often the panel decides the cost of what is needed to repair the damage, which can include whether cleaning will actually work. Policy wording affects the details.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is lightning damage covered?</summary><div class="a"><p>Lightning is a named peril in most homeowners policies. Damage to electronics and wiring may be included, subject to the policy terms.</p></div></details><details><summary>Should I wait for the fire investigation before demanding appraisal?</summary><div class="a"><p>If the insurer is still investigating cause or coverage, it is usually wise to let that finish and to talk with a South Carolina attorney first.</p></div></details></div>
